      "problem": "What is the center of the hyperbola $\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{36}-\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}=1?$",
      "worked_solution": "The equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that the given equation can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{{6}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-(-1))^{2}}{{2}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $h=-1$ and $k=5.$\n\nTherefore, the center of the hyperbola is $(-1,5).$",
